class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> fourSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        //Optimal
        /*
        Time Complexity: O(N3), where N is the size of the given array.
Sorting the array takes O(NlogN) time, and the 3 nested loops take O(N3) time. Thus, the overall time complexity is O(N3) + O(NlogN), which boils down to O(N3).

Space Complexity: O(no. of quadruplets), this space is only used to store the answer. No extra space is used to solve this problem. So, from that perspective, space complexity can be written as O(1).
        */
        int n = nums.length;
        List<List<Integer>> quar = new LinkedList<List<Integer>>();
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        for(int i =0;i<n;i++) {
        	if(i>0 && nums[i] == nums[i-1]) continue;
        	for(int j =i+1;j<n;j++) {
        		if(j>i+1 && nums[j] == nums[j-1] ) continue;
        		int k = j+1;
        		int m = n-1;
                while(k<m){
                    long sum  = (long)nums[i]+nums[j]+nums[k]+nums[m];
        			if(sum == target) {
        				List<Integer> list = new LinkedList<Integer>();
        				list.add(nums[i]);
        				list.add(nums[j]);
        				list.add(nums[k]);
        				list.add(nums[m]);
        				quar.add(list);
        				k++;
        				m--;
        				while(k<m && nums[k] == nums[k-1])k++;
        				while(k<m && nums[m]== nums[m+1])m--;
          			}
        			else if(sum<target) {
        				k++;
        			}else {
        				m--;
        			}
                }
        			
        		}
        }
        
    	return quar;
        //Better TC = O(N3*log(M)) for using 3 nested loops and inside the loops there are some operations on the set data structure which take log(M) time complexity, where N is size of the array, M is number of elements in the set.
